<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Qarmatians of Bahrain</h1><p class="st-meta">899 CE – 1077 CE (approx.) · capital al-Ahsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A radical Isma'ili state in eastern Arabia, ruled by a council at al-Ahsa, that stole the Black Stone from Mecca in 930.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 899 the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-al-jannabi">Abu Sa'id al-Jannabi</a>, preaching the coming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, won over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> and peasants in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, then called <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a>, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Ahsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a>. His state broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate's</a> claims to leadership and became a separate Qarmatian power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-al-jannabi">Abu Sa'id</a>, the state was governed by a council of his descendants and leading men. Visitors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-khusraw">Nasir Khusraw</a> reported that citizens paid no taxes, debts were interest free and thousands of African slaves worked the date groves. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-tahir-al-jannabi">Abu Tahir</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, attacked pilgrim caravans and in 930 sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, carrying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Ahsa</a> until 951.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Their power declined after defeats by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. In 1077 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-uyuni">Abd Allah al-Uyuni</a>, a local chief, overthrew them with help from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uyunid-emirate">Uyunid Emirate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 899 CE – 1077 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.59%;width:3.54%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>899 CE – 1077 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>al-Ahsa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Sa'id al-Jannabi, Abu Tahir al-Jannabi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Qarmatian Isma'ili Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, <a href="/countries/kuwait">Kuwait</a>, <a href="/countries/qatar">Qatar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qarmatian state, Qaramita, Jannabid state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=988&amp;focus=qarmatians-of-bahrain" aria-label="Qarmatians of Bahrain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5872 1292 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qarmatians of Bahrain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M61